.\" cut.1 -- maux's cut(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original
.\" page (this project's own text, not copied from a historic
.\" cut(1)), so it carries no third-party notice -- MIT like the
.\" code, same posture as head.1. Rendered at build time to
.\" build/man/CUT.TXT and staged on the play image as
.\" /usr/man/CUT: `more /usr/man/CUT`.
.TH CUT 1 "September 14, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
cut \- cut out selected fields of each line of a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cut
.B \-b
.I list
[
.B \-n
] [ file ] ...
.br
.B cut
.B \-c
.I list
[ file ] ...
.br
.B cut
.B \-f
.I list
[
.BI \-d " delim"
] [
.B \-s
] [ file ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cut
selects portions of each line of each named
.I file
(standard input default) and writes them to the standard
output.  Exactly one of
.BR \-b ", " \-c ", " \-f
must be given.
.PP
.I List
is a comma-separated list of numbers and number ranges,
one-based:
.I N
selects one position,
.I N\-M
a range,
.I N\-
everything from
.I N
to the end of the line, and
.I \-M
everything from the start of the line through
.IR M .
The positions or fields named need not be in order, and may
overlap; each is still selected only once, and the output
carries them in the order they appear in the line, not the
order they appear in
.IR list .
.PP
.B \-b
and
.B \-c
select
.I list
as byte or character positions.  This machine has no multibyte
characters, so the two are the same operation, and
.B \-n
(which asks that a multibyte character not be split) is
accepted with
.B \-b
but has nothing to do.
.PP
.B \-f
selects
.I list
as delimiter-separated fields, the default delimiter being
.BR <tab> .
.BI \-d " delim"
names a different delimiter, exactly one character.  The
delimiter separating two selected fields in the output is the
same byte as the input delimiter.
.PP
A line containing no delimiter byte is written unchanged,
.I list
notwithstanding, unless
.B \-s
is given, in which case it is suppressed entirely.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/CUT  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
grep(1), sed(1), tr(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0; 1 if a file named on the command line could
not be opened -- that file is skipped, a diagnostic naming it
is written to standard error, and the remaining operands are
still processed; 2 for a usage error, including a missing or
duplicated mode flag, a malformed
.IR list ", or a " \-d
argument that is not exactly one character.
.SH BUGS
A line longer than 255 bytes is truncated at that length before
.I list
is applied.
